# NMEA 0183 sentences are terminated by CR LF, and the AIS fixtures are compared byte for byte
# against golden output. Line-ending normalisation would rewrite them on checkout on Windows
# and every round-trip test would fail on a difference no reader could see in a diff.
*.nmea -text

# The same rule for ADS-B frames, and for the same reason one step removed. These files are LF
# and the emitter produces LF, so a checkout that rewrote them to CRLF would fail
# test_the_ingest_round_trip_is_byte_exact on a difference no reader could see in a diff —
# `_parse_frames` tolerates CRLF on the way in, which is exactly what would make the failure
# look like an adapter defect rather than a checkout artefact.
*.adsb -text

# GMTI packets are raw binary — 32 bytes of header and length-prefixed segments — and
# test_every_fixture_round_trips_byte_for_byte compares them byte for byte against what the
# adapter emits. Git would classify them as binary on its own (they contain NULs), but the
# marker is explicit because the consequence of being wrong is a failure that reads as an
# adapter defect rather than as a checkout artefact.
*.gmti -text -diff

# The three ASTERIX categories, raw data blocks, and the marker is explicit for the same reason
# `.gmti`'s is: they contain NULs so git classifies them as binary on its own, and being wrong
# about that produces a byte-exact round-trip failure that reads as an adapter defect rather than
# as a checkout artefact.
#
# They were missing here, and the omission has a twin. The SDK round found the built wheel short
# by 72 files — every `.cat048` and `.cat034` block — because `pyproject.toml` listed the
# extensions it shipped and two adapters had landed since the list was written. This file listed
# the extensions it protected, and the same two were missing from it. One habit, two enumerations,
# the same two gaps. The packaging list is now a recursive glob with a gate behind it; this one
# cannot be, because a path attribute IS a pattern, so it stays a list and is stated here.
*.cat021 -text -diff
*.cat034 -text -diff
*.cat048 -text -diff

# The fourth ASTERIX category, raw data blocks, and the marker is explicit for the same reason the
# first three are. Added in the SAME COMMIT as the adapter that produces the blocks, which is the
# habit the paragraph above exists to establish: the two enumerations that went stale did so
# because the extension arrived one round after the fixtures did.
*.cat062 -text -diff

# The fifth ASTERIX category, and the last of this round. Same reason, same habit: the extension
# arrives in the commit that produces the blocks.
*.cat023 -text -diff

# The KLV family and the STANAG 4586 messages, and the marker is explicit for the reason every
# entry above gives. They were missing here from the day their fixtures landed — 65 `.klv`, 30
# `.imapb`, 26 `.klvframe` and 14 `.s4586` files by 2026-09-16 — and git's NUL heuristic carried
# them, which is exactly what the `.gmti` paragraph says not to rely on. Nine of them are the case
# the heuristic gets wrong: one-to-three-octet framing and IMAPB payloads with no NUL in them, which
# git classifies as text with no line ending, so the next such fixture that happens to hold 0x0A
# would be rewritten to CRLF on a Windows checkout and fail the byte-exact assertions in
# `tests/test_cdm_klv_framing.py` and `tests/test_cdm_stanag4586_adapter.py` as an adapter defect.
# Added 2026-09-16, four extensions and several rounds late, which is the habit the ASTERIX
# paragraph was written to end.
*.klv -text -diff
*.imapb -text -diff
*.klvframe -text -diff
*.s4586 -text -diff
